{
	title: 'Attachments: images and long pastes reach the model',
	description: `A prompt can carry attachments. A client sends each once with an \`attach\` command { sessionId, mediaType, data (base64), name? }; the host checks it (png, jpeg, gif or webp images, or text/plain; at most 5 MB decoded; refused otherwise with a reason), stores it as sessions/<id>/blobs/<blobId>.<ext> with a random blob id, and answers that client with an \`attached\` event naming the command and the blob. The prompt text holds a marker for it: [image <blobId>] or [paste <blobId>, N lines]. When a prompt is submitted, the host resolves the markers of that session's blobs: an image becomes an image block { type: 'image', blob, mediaType } in the user record, and a paste becomes its text. History holds blob references, never the bytes. Providers read the bytes when they build a request: Anthropic as a base64 image source, OpenAI-compatible as an image_url data URL; replay and amend keep image blocks. A marker naming no blob of the session stays text, and the host tells the client.
Clients show an image block as a line like [image 12 kB png] in the terminal and as the image in the web, served by an authenticated GET /blob/<sessionId>/<blobId> (the login cookie; only blobs of that session, only by exact id; no path from the request reaches the file system).
Tests: an attached png reaches both providers' request bodies as an image; history has no base64; a forged marker or a blob of another session is not resolved; the web route refuses a missing cookie and a path with slashes or dots.`,
	status: 'planned',
	needs: [
		'yq',
		'1b'
	],
	notes: [
		'old-attachments.ts resolved [path.png] markers pointing anywhere on disk and let .txt markers read files under /tmp/hal. hal2 must not: only blobs the host stored for that session resolve. old-blob.ts and old-web-upload.ts are reference for storage and upload limits.',
		'The Anthropic API limits images to about 5 MB each and some OpenAI-compatible endpoints reject images; a provider without image support should get a text note in place of the image, not a failed request.',
		'Protocol messages are ASON over the socket and the WebSocket; a 5 MB base64 string is one large message. Measure that the socket framing and ason.parse handle it before choosing a smaller cap or chunking.'
	]
}
